WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.549
A plan can contain strong environmental-justice language and still fail if actions are unfunded, delayed, or impossible to track.

2
00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:14.981
Prioritized improvements, implementation responsibility, budgets, and public reporting turn policy into an accountable program.

3
00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:26.860
This lesson draws from California DOJ's Best Practices for Implementing SB one thousand, the DOJ's current SB one thousand page, and current Government Code section sixty-five thousand three hundred two.

4
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:30.079
It is independent study, not legal or financial advice.

5
00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:32.225
Prioritization should be explicit.

6
00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:43.273
Possible criteria include severity, number of people affected, compounded risk, community priority, feasibility, readiness, co-benefits, historic investment gaps, and urgency.

7
00:00:43.273 --> 00:00:45.356
Publish the criteria and weights.

8
00:00:45.356 --> 00:00:49.144
Test whether reasonable changes produce a different ranking.

9
00:00:49.144 --> 00:00:51.669
Do not let readiness always defeat need.

10
00:00:51.669 --> 00:00:57.414
A project can appear ready because a better-resourced community had capacity to prepare it.

11
00:00:57.414 --> 00:01:03.096
Consider technical assistance and early funding that make high-need actions implementable.

12
00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:05.215
Create an implementation register.

13
00:01:05.215 --> 00:01:16.307
For each action, record the lead, partners, authority, capital and operating cost, funding status, start and completion dates, output, outcome, geography, and correction trigger.

14
00:01:16.307 --> 00:01:19.609
Distinguish a grant application from secured funding.

15
00:01:19.609 --> 00:01:21.977
Report distribution as well as totals.

16
00:01:21.977 --> 00:01:26.776
Citywide spending can rise while priority communities receive little benefit.

17
00:01:26.776 --> 00:01:31.075
Track whether outcomes improve, not only whether tasks are completed.

18
00:01:31.075 --> 00:01:33.568
Invite community review of the measures.

19
00:01:33.568 --> 00:01:35.255
Torrance application segment.

20
00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:41.710
This original segment cites the City of Torrance General Plan page, checked October seventh, twenty twenty-six.

21
00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:48.049
For a hypothetical environmental-justice implementation program, rank three actions using published criteria.

22
00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:51.481
Link each to a budget pathway and operating responsibility.

23
00:01:51.481 --> 00:01:57.820
Create a public dashboard outline that shows status, spending, geography, outcome, and explanation for delay.

24
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.473
Add an annual community review and a trigger for revising an ineffective action.

25
00:02:02.473 --> 00:02:08.870
This exercise does not describe Torrance's budget, establish priorities for the City, or determine compliance.

26
00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:20.468
Accountability means that the public can see what was promised, who owns it, what resources exist, what happened, who benefited, and how the plan will change if results fall short.

27
00:02:20.468 --> 00:02:24.656
DOJ state-authored source text is used as public-domain material.

28
00:02:24.656 --> 00:02:28.651
Third-party and local-government appendix content is excluded.

29
00:02:28.651 --> 00:02:31.099
No DOJ or City endorsement is implied.
